    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The diagram shows the inner mitochondrial membrane with Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase), Complex II (Succinate dehydrogenase), Complex III (Cytochrome \(bc_1\)), and four masked components: <strong>I</strong> receives electrons from both Complex I and Complex II and passes them to Complex III; <strong>II</strong> is attached to the outer surface of the inner membrane between Complex III and <strong>III</strong>; <strong>III</strong> receives electrons from <strong>II</strong> and transfers them to <strong>IV</strong> along with \(2\text{H}^+\) to form \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Sign Conventions</strong><br/>Electron flow proceeds sequentially along the carriers in order of increasing redox potential toward molecular oxygen.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Formulae and Principles</strong><br/>In the mitochondrial ETS:<br/>- Ubiquinone (UQ) within the inner membrane receives reducing equivalents from both Complex I and Complex II, becoming ubiquinol (\(\text{UQH}_2\)), and transfers electrons to Complex III.<br/>- Cytochrome \(c\) is a small protein attached to the outer surface of the inner membrane that acts as a mobile carrier between Complex III and Complex IV.<br/>- Complex IV is cytochrome \(c\) oxidase (containing cytochromes \(a\) and \(a_3\), and two copper centres), which passes electrons to the terminal acceptor \(\frac{1}{2}\text{O}_2\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>1. Component <strong>I</strong> receives electrons from both Complex I and Complex II and passes them to Complex III, so <strong>I</strong> is Ubiquinone (UQ / \(\text{UQH}_2\)).<br/>2. Component <strong>II</strong> lies on the outer surface of the inner membrane between Complex III and <strong>III</strong>, so <strong>II</strong> is Cytochrome \(c\).<br/>3. Component <strong>III</strong> is the terminal complex receiving electrons from Cytochrome \(c\), so <strong>III</strong> is Complex IV (Cytochrome \(c\) oxidase).<br/>4. Component <strong>IV</strong> combines with \(2\text{H}^+\) and electrons at Complex IV to form \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), so <strong>IV</strong> is \(\frac{1}{2}\text{O}_2\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>All carriers and complexes match the standard mitochondrial electron transport chain. Therefore, <strong>I</strong> = Ubiquinone, <strong>II</strong> = Cytochrome \(c\), <strong>III</strong> = Complex IV (Cytochrome \(c\) oxidase), and <strong>IV</strong> = \(\frac{1}{2}\text{O}_2\).</p>
